rn-fritter"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Dadiah","dish/dadiah"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Emping","dish/emping"],["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Geplak","dish/geplak"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/26/Lemper.jpg/500px-Lemper.jpg","w":500,"h":332,"by":"Hariadhi","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lemper.jpg"}},"ingredient/kangkung":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/kangkung","n":"Kangkung","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Ipomoea aquatica, commonly known as water spinach or kangkung,  is a semi-aquatic, tropical plant grown as a vegetable for its tender shoots. I. aquatica is generally believed to have been first domesticated in Southeast Asia. It is widely cultivated in Southeast Asia, East Asia, and South Asia. It grows abundantly near waterways and requires little to no care."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-kangkung"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kangkung"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Balinese cuisine","cuisine/balinese-cuisine",[["Plecing kangkung","dish/plecing-kangkung"]]],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Plecing kangkung","dish/plecing-kangkung"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Ipomoea aquatica","ingredient/ipomoea-aquatica"],["Mung bean sprout","ingredient/mung-bean-sprout"],["Sambal","ingredient/sambal"],["Shrimp paste","ingredient/shrimp-paste"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/10/N_Ipoa_D1600.JPG/500px-N_Ipoa_D1600.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Eric Guinther","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N_Ipoa_D1600.JPG"}},"ingredient/salvia":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/salvia","n":"Salvia","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Salvia () is the largest genus of plants in the sage family Lamiaceae, with just under 1,000 species of shrubs, herbaceous perennials, and annuals. Within the Lamiaceae, Salvia is part of the tribe Mentheae within the subfamily Nepetoideae.  One of several genera commonly referred to as sage, it includes two widely used herbs, Salvia officinalis (common sage, or just \"sage\") and Salvia rosmarinus (rosemary, formerly Rosmarinus officinalis)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-salvia"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salvia"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Saltimbocca","dish/saltimbocca"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Prosciutto","ingredient/prosciutto"],["Salvia officinalis","ingredient/salvia-officinalis"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5a/Salvia_officinalis0.jpg/500px-Salvia_officinalis0.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Kurt Stüber [1]","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salvia_officinalis0.jpg"}},"dish/stracciatella-ice-cream":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/stracciatella-ice-cream","n":"Stracciatella (ice cream)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Italian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Stracciatella is a variety of gelato with fine strands of drizzled chocolate stirred through it. It was originally created in Bergamo, northern Italy, at the Ristorante La Marianna in 1961. Stracciatella was inspired by stracciatella soup, made from broth into which beaten egg is drizzled, popular around Rome."]]